{"units": [{"id": "kataitho-word5", "translation": "you seem to be burning me"}, {"id": "kataitho-word6", "translation": "you seem to be burning me (P)"}, {"id": "kataitho-word7", "translation": "you seem to be burning me (B)"}, {"id": "kataitho-word8", "translation": "you seem to be burning me"}, {"id": "kataitho-word9", "translation": "you seem to be burning me"}, {"id": "kataitho-word10", "translation": "you indeed gave birth to me, you seem to be destroying me"}, {"id": "kataitho-word11", "translation": "to destroy"}, {"id": "kataineo-orth1", "translation": "to promise"}, {"id": "kataineo-def1", "translation": "to promise"}, {"id": "kataineo-quote1", "translation": "having promised and having been received as a guest"}, {"id": "kataisios-orth1", "translation": "fitting, proper"}, {"id": "kataisios-def1", "translation": "fitting, proper"}, {"id": "kataisios-note1", "translation": "A word not known elsewhere, except that Hesychius interpreted καταίσια [fitting things] by καταίσιμα [fitting things]."}, {"id": "kataisios-quote1", "translation": "recognizing a deed not unfitting"}, {"id": "kataisios-word1", "translation": "fitting things"}, {"id": "kataisios-word2", "translation": "fitting things"}, {"id": "kataischynter-orth1", "translation": "one who disgraces"}, {"id": "kataischynter-def1", "translation": "one who disgraces"}, {"id": "kataischynter-quote1", "translation": "shall we yield to these leaders who disgrace the house?"}, {"id": "kataischyno-orth1", "translation": "to disgrace"}, {"id": "kataischyno-def1", "translation": "to disgrace"}, {"id": "kataischyno-quote1", "translation": "not to disgrace the path of the long journey"}, {"id": "kataischyno-quote2", "translation": "I praise you for not disgracing me"}, {"id": "katakalyptho-orth1", "translation": "to cover over"}, {"id": "katakalyptho-def1", "translation": "to cover over"}, {"id": "katakalyptho-note1", "translation": "By tmesis."}, {"id": "katakalyptho-quote1", "translation": "Would that, Zeus, he had covered me too by the fate of death"}, {"id": "katakarphomai-orth1", "translation": "to wither away"}, {"id": "katakarphomai-def1", "translation": "to wither away"}, {"id": "katakarphomai-note1", "translation": "The use of the simple verb κάρφομαι [I wither] is more frequent."}, {"id": "katakarphomai-quote1", "translation": "the foliage already withering"}, {"id": "katakarphomai-quote2", "translation": "κατακάρφει [it withers]: it destroys, it dries up. κατακάρψεις [you will wither]: you will burn up."}, {"id": "katakarphomai-word1", "translation": "I wither"}, {"id": "kataklyzo-orth1", "translation": "to flood"}, {"id": "kataklyzo-def1", "translation": "to flood"}, {"id": "kataklyzo-note1", "translation": "Thus L. Dindorf for κατακλυσθῆναι τὰ μάλιστα [to be flooded most of all]. On the apocope of the infinitive see under χρίμπτω [to bring near]."}, {"id": "kataklyzo-quote1", "translation": "to be flooded by a wave of foreign men"}, {"id": "kataklyzo-word1", "translation": "to be flooded most of all"}, {"id": "kataklyzo-word2", "translation": "to bring near"}, {"id": "katakrateo-orth1", "translation": "to hold power"}, {"id": "katakrateo-def1", "translation": "to hold power"}, {"id": "katakrateo-quote1", "translation": "for by divine fate it prevailed"}, {"id": "katakrypto-orth1", "translation": "to conceal"}, {"id": "katakrypto-def1", "translation": "to conceal"}, {"id": "katakrypto-quote1", "translation": "the city of Susa—you hid it in dark grief"}, {"id": "katakteino-orth1", "translation": "to kill"}, {"id": "katakteino-def1", "translation": "to kill"}, {"id": "katakteino-note1", "translation": "where θάνες κατακτάς [you died having killed] Lachmann, deleting σὺ δ' [but you]."}, {"id": "katakteino-quote1", "translation": "but you died having killed"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote2", "translation": "you lie dead having killed"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote3", "translation": "nor did he come as he killed"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote4", "translation": "but you say that you killed this man willingly"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote5", "translation": "you indeed will kill yourself, not I"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote6", "translation": "but my black-hearted mother killed him"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote7", "translation": "tell me first if you killed my mother"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote8", "translation": "but indeed you must say how you killed"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote9", "translation": "she killed my father, murdering a man"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote10", "translation": "he was not a kinsman of the man he killed"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote11", "translation": "if I killed them justly"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote12", "translation": "Argos, the son of Hermes, killed him"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote13", "translation": "for what harm did you kill my son?"}, {"id": "katakteino-quote14", "translation": "Zeus who killed"}, {"id": "katakteino-word1", "translation": "you died having killed"}, {"id": "katakteino-word2", "translation": "but you"}, {"id": "kataleipo-orth1", "translation": "to leave behind"}, {"id": "kataleipo-def1", "translation": "to leave behind"}, {"id": "kataleipo-note1", "translation": "correctly μοι [to me] in the Florentine manuscript in Miller p. 112."}, {"id": "kataleipo-quote1", "translation": "Capaneus is left behind to me"}, {"id": "kataleipo-word1", "translation": "to me"}, {"id": "kataleo-orth1", "translation": "to cease, to stop, to come to an end"}, {"id": "kataleo-def1", "translation": "to cease, to stop, to come to an end"}, {"id": "kataleo-quote1", "translation": "before the ancient grief ceases"}, {"id": "kataleo-quote2", "translation": "where will the transferred fury of ruin cease?"}, {"id": "katallage-orth1", "translation": "reconciliation"}, {"id": "katallage-def1", "translation": "reconciliation"}, {"id": "katallage-note1", "translation": "Scholium: δυσχερὲς τὸ φιλιωθῆναι αὐτοὺς διὰ τὰς κατάρας [it is difficult for them to be reconciled because of the curses]."}, {"id": "katallage-quote1", "translation": "heavy reconciliations"}, {"id": "katallage-word1", "translation": "it is difficult for them to be reconciled because of the curses"}, {"id": "katamenyo-orth1", "translation": "to reveal"}, {"id": "katamenyo-def1", "translation": "to reveal"}, {"id": "katamenyo-quote1", "translation": "I will reveal this"}, {"id": "katamophos-orth1", "translation": "blameworthy"}, {"id": "katamophos-def1", "translation": "blameworthy"}, {"id": "katamophos-quote1", "translation": "showing favorable but blameworthy omens"}, {"id": "katanao-orth1", "translation": "to settle in a home"}, {"id": "katanao-def1", "translation": "to settle in a home"}, {"id": "katanao-def2", "translation": "middle form"}, {"id": "katanao-note1", "translation": "An unusual present form, whose aorist κατένασσα [I settled] is found in Hesiod Op. 166 and others indicated in the Thesaurus vol. 4 p. 1152 and by Veitch p. 405 third edition."}, {"id": "katanao-quote1", "translation": "having settled there the hard-to-please spirits"}, {"id": "katanao-word1", "translation": "I settled"}, {"id": "katanthrakoomai-orth1", "translation": "to turn into charcoal"}, {"id": "katanthrakoomai-def1", "translation": "to turn into charcoal"}, {"id": "katanthrakoomai-quote1", "translation": "I will set fire to the roof and turn it into charcoal"}, {"id": "kataxaino-orth1", "translation": "to wear away, to consume"}, {"id": "kataxaino-def1", "translation": "to wear away, to consume"}, {"id": "kataxaino-quote1", "translation": "and the breezes coming from the Strymon, making a long time, wore away the flower of the Argives with friction"}, {"id": "kataxeno-orth1", "translation": "to receive as a guest"}, {"id": "kataxeno-def1", "translation": "to receive as a guest"}, {"id": "kataxeno-quote1", "translation": "having promised and having been received as a guest"}, {"id": "kataxioo-orth1", "translation": "to deem worthy"}]}